    "Federated learning and multi-agent systems aggregate contributions whose behaviour the aggregator cannot inspect. Defences rely on a clean root dataset or on cohort statistics that presuppose an honest majority. "
    "For forecasting tasks in which the direction of the output for a controlled input follows from a domain rule, we study Probe-Trust: the server sends each submitted model synthetic probe windows (speed must not rise in a congestion probe nor collapse in a free-flow probe), measures the violation of this absolute rule, and converts it into a soft aggregation weight. "
    f"We measure harm on honest nodes during real congestion events on METR-LA, PEMS-BAY and PeMS-D7(M) ({N['final_runs']:,} runs, five to twenty seeds). With 60% attackers, two-sided Probe-Trust keeps the harm at {mx(PD6['probe2'], ('hide','exagg','bias')):+.1f}% or less on hide, false-congestion and bias attacks, where Krum reaches {T06['krum']['hide']:+.0f}% to {T06['krum']['bias']:+.0f}%; scoring the same probes relative to the other clients gives {NA['attack']['probe2_rel']['hide']['0.6']['median']:+.0f}%. "
    "The result holds under strong non-IID data and for transformer, perceptron and graph-augmented forecasters without retuning. Update flipping and trigger backdoors are not stopped without a clean-root check, and against FLTrust with a clean root the gain is small. "
    "Limits: a classifier separates our synthetic probes from real windows (AUC above 0.95), so a probe-detecting adversary remains possible; probes from public history are harder to detect but protect less against bias; on solar power forecasting two of three attacks are stopped. "
