P(f"Zeno++ and FLAME (Table {tzn}). We added two reference methods that reviewers asked for, both reimplemented from the papers because we found no official code for Zeno++ or FLAME {C('zeno', 'flame')}. Zeno++ clips each update to the norm of the server's own update and accepts it if its inner product with that update exceeds a fraction (0.1) of its squared norm; the threshold was not tuned. FLAME clusters the update directions with HDBSCAN, clips the norms to their median and adds noise of scale 0.001 times the median norm. At 60% attackers the FLAME variants fail on all five attacks ({fm(R4['hide|0.6|flame_full']['median'])}% on hide, {fm(R4['exagg|0.6|flame_full']['median'])}% on false congestion, {fm(R4['flip|0.6|flame_full']['median'])}% on flipping), because the largest cluster is the attackers, as expected without an honest majority. Zeno++ behaves like FLTrust: it stays within {max(R4[f'{a}|0.6|zenopp']['median'] for a in ('hide','exagg','bias','flip','alie')):.1f}% on all attacks, including flipping, which the probes alone do not stop. It uses a clean update on the server, like FLTrust, and on hide it is a few points behind Probe-Trust ({R4['hide|0.6|zenopp']['median']:+.1f}% against {T06['probe2']['hide']:+.1f}%). This confirms the main limit of the work: when a trusted root is available, root-based rules are as good as the probes, and the probes are useful where it is not.")
