P(f"A second domain: solar power forecasting (Table {tsl}). To test whether the idea is tied to traffic, we used the NREL Solar-Energy data distributed with LSTNet {C('lstnet')}: 137 photovoltaic plants in Alabama, 10-minute power for 2006, one client per plant, the same 12-step input and 3-step forecast, the same simulator and the same thresholds (delta = 0.5σ, tau = 0.1σ), without any tuning. Night values (zero) are treated as missing. The physical rule is the same: when the output is low, the forecast must not jump up by more than delta (a hidden shortfall), and when the output is high and steady it must not collapse (a false drop); probe ranges are the 2nd–10th and 60th–95th percentiles of the daytime output; events are midday (9:00–15:00) dips below 65% of the typical output with at least three of four correlated neighbours also low. The attacks shift the labels by 20 MW (about 1.8σ) when the last output is below the 15th percentile (hide), above the median (false drop) or always (bias). Probe-Trust handles the false drop and bias attacks ({fm(SL['exagg|0.6|probe2']['median'])}% and {fm(SL['bias|0.6|probe2']['median'])}% at 60%, against {fm(SL['exagg|0.6|fedavg']['median'])}% and {fm(SL['bias|0.6|fedavg']['median'])}% for FedAvg), but not the hide attack: {fm(SL['hide|0.4|probe2']['median'])}% at 40% and {fm(SL['hide|0.6|probe2']['median'])}% at 60%, no better than FedAvg ({fm(SL['hide|0.4|fedavg']['median'])}% and {fm(SL['hide|0.6|fedavg']['median'])}%). The weights separate the hide attackers from honest plants only partly (AUC {RV['S_auc']['hide|0.4']:.2f} at 40% and {RV['S_auc']['hide|0.6']:.2f} at 60%, against {RV['S_auc']['exagg|0.6']:.2f} for the false drop), so even a good ranking does not remove their influence; a plausible but untested reason is that honest forecasters also raise their forecast from a low output at sunrise and after clouds, so the rule is violated by honest models too. With the thresholds set after the fact, a tighter delta = 0.1σ lowers the harm of hide to {fm(SDL['0.1']['probe2|0.4'])}% and {fm(SDL['0.1']['probe2|0.6'])}% at a cost of {fm(SDL['0.1']['clean_probe2'])}% on clean runs; this was chosen on the same data and is not an independent result. FLTrust and the combination handle all three attacks (at most {fm(max(SL[f'{a}|{sh}|{m}']['median'] for a in ('hide','exagg','bias') for sh in ('0.4','0.6') for m in ('fltrust','probe2_fl')))}%). Only one dataset was used, so no between-dataset spread is available; the transfer is partial: the mechanism works where the rule is physically one-sided (a clear sky cannot produce a sudden surplus, a false drop is detectable) and needs a domain-specific design where the honest dynamics break the rule.")
