# ======================== VIII. DISCUSSION ========================
H1("Discussion")
P(f"Probe-Trust trades generality for verifiability: it needs a physical rule for the output on a controlled input. Where such a rule exists, it provides an absolute score that remains valid when attackers outnumber honest clients, which cohort-relative scoring {C('uav')} and distance-based rules cannot offer, and it needs no clean data, which root-of-trust defences {C('fltrust', 'zeno')} do. Against FLTrust with a good root, the measured gain is small; the practical value is robustness to the quality of the root and the freedom from trusted data. The probes are a form of known-answer testing and, for agents, of behavioural attestation: a participant is trusted to the extent that its answers on controlled inputs obey known constraints.")
P(f"Limitations. The cohort-relative, FedMID-style, FLAME and Zeno++ variants are our own implementations, not the authors' code (we found none for Zeno++ and FLAME). We did not compare experimentally with generator-based or learned probing {C('zafar', 'fedfg', 'specshield')} or with learned reputation schemes {C('rohfl', 'securegraphfl', 'flare', 'traftrust')}, which address different tasks and have no released traffic implementation; the evidence is limited to the baselines of Table II. The physical rule must be specified per domain, probes that are indistinguishable from real inputs were not studied, and only small neural models and eight LLMs were used. Future work: graph forecasters, learned probe generation for other cyber-physical tasks, and engineered adaptive adversaries.")

# ======================== IX. CONCLUSION ========================
H1("Conclusion")
P(f"Rule-based probe windows give an absolute, label-free and cohort-free score of submitted forecasters. On three traffic datasets they keep the harm on honest nodes in events at {mx(PD6['probe2'], ('hide','exagg','bias')):+.1f}% or less with 60% attackers on hide, false-congestion and bias attacks, require a two-sided rule, and remove the dependence on a clean root, but they do not stop update flipping or trigger backdoors without a root-based check. The guarantee is empirical and conditional: shifts below the dead zone of the rule (about 6 mph) are not prevented, and the probes must lie in the regime that attackers manipulate (milder congestion probes let the hide attack through). The same principle separated compromised LLM agents in a controlled numeric-consensus experiment. The probes are easy to tell from real windows, which leaves a detector-gated adversary open, and on solar power forecasting only two of the three attacks were stopped. The main open questions are engineered probe-aware adversaries and probes that are both hidden and discriminative, spatio-temporal graph forecasters, further domains, and a head-to-head comparison with learned probing.")
H1("Data and Code Availability", num=False)
